On Wed, 5 Oct 2005, Ariamaki wrote:

> Compiling:  Where exactly does one begin compiling this collection of
> files into the single batched pack we normally see?  I am not
> attempting to over-step my boundries here, simply wondering how I would
> go about making a working version after I made any additions, so I
> could test them.

First, make sure that that you can create a playable game /without/ any
modifications (documented in the source distribution). Then, the process
to create a game with small changes is the same.

[using Windows]
> 2- I already have a good C editor, what I really need is a good
> COMPILER....  Because the only compiler I have access to that I know of
> is the one imbedded in my C++ workset at school...

The compilers which are supported by NetHack for Windows are listed in the
documentation. Of those, BCC 5.5.1 and MinGW 2.0 can be downloaded freely,
and Microsoft's compiler can be downloaded freely in a later version.

In any case, before you start to implement additions, get a feeling for
the source. For example, place a breakpoint at an interesting line (say,
drinking a potion), and then go step-by-step through the code to see
/what/ NetHack does (You may not necessarily understand exactly /how/, but
the names of functions and variables will give you an indication of what
is going on.).

